# AI engineering workflow policy

Codex is the default engineering runtime. This repository owns workflow policy,
not the host agent loop, sandbox, approvals, or tools. Optional integrations must
never become a required hop for normal work.

Use the minimum justified process. For an explicit resume request, validate
`ai-workflow/state/active.md` first and continue its exact `Resume target` with
the matching `workflow-*` skill. Otherwise route in this order:

1. Honor an explicit learning request or named supported workflow.
2. Use `workflow-teach` for a stated knowledge gap blocking safe progress.
3. Use `workflow-discovery` when materially different unresolved choices affect
   architecture, security, cost, dependencies, or visible behavior.
4. Use `workflow-debugging` for an existing unexplained failure; diagnosis does
   not authorize a fix.
5. For approved work, use `workflow-decomposition` only when multiple
   dependency-ordered or independently deliverable sessions add value;
   otherwise build one coherent scope with `workflow-implementation`.
6. After meaningful work, use `workflow-verification` for executable evidence,
   then `workflow-review` when an independent pass materially raises confidence.
7. Handle a clear, bounded, low-risk request directly.

Risk, uncertainty, reversibility, and intent matter more than diff size. Read the
project profile only when project facts or commands are needed. Never invent a
project command. Apply its command safety contract; every external-scope,
explicitly approval-required, externally mutating, or destructive action needs
specific authorization. Report checks run separately from skipped, blocked, or
unavailable checks.

Use one capable parent by default. Use host-native subagents only for bounded
independent work when isolation or parallelism materially helps. The parent owns
synthesis, decisions, edits, finding disposition, and final verification.

The project-owned specification and local Implementation and Verification
workflows remain canonical. Optional upstream skills are explicit and fail
closed when unavailable; `/to-spec` and `/implement` do not replace these local
routes. Native ticket artifacts remain canonical and are never mirrored. The
framework never requires or delegates through an external agent runtime.

Accepted repository decision/state outranks active state, private agent memory,
and chat recollection. Private learning is never project truth. Promotion into
shared policy, skills, profiles, decisions, or state is a separate reviewable
change requiring reusable evidence, duplication/staleness review, and the
narrowest scope. Never persist raw transcripts, secrets, credentials, or
unnecessary personal data.
